{
  "configuration_id": "config_020",
  "reason": "Evidence: config_018 (multimodal, multiprofile, pca_n2000, resnet50, avgpool, concat) achieved the best result (acc=0.619, F1=0.610), showing a large gain over the singleprofile baseline (config_016, acc=0.527). This jump was driven by the multiprofile+avgpool combination, but we have not yet tested whether a more expressive aggregation (transformer) can further improve over simple average pooling. Question: config_020 changes only the aggregation from avgpool to transformer while holding all other settings (multimodal, multiprofile, pca_n2000, resnet50, concat fusion) identical to the current best. This is a clean single-variable test of whether the aggregation strategy is at its ceiling or whether capturing inter-profile interactions via a transformer yields additional gains. Why this is the best next experiment: the aggregation method is the most fundamental architectural decision in the multiprofile pipeline, and resolving it early (before exploring RNA encoder variants, image encoder variants, or fusion strategies) maximizes information gain per experiment under the limited budget. If transformer outperforms avgpool, it opens a new promising axis; if not, we confirm avgpool sufficiency and can redirect effort to other design dimensions."
}

